requirements.realtime_layer:
MUST stay on the MCU. This is a Teensy 4.1-class board, already budgeted at 4 W and 0.18/0.24 kg (hardware/trex_form_study/designlab/build.py:465-471; designlab/power.py:33-37). It owns everything whose timing the policy was trained on, and everything that must work when Linux is late or dead:
(1) The CAN exchange with the 12 leg drives on two buses, plus the expression servos (TTL bridge at 60-100 cm, RobStride neck on CAN at 120 cm), with per-axis position/speed/torque/temperature limits. The drives were modelled as position servos with an 8 ms command delay and 50 Hz zero-order hold (PR e103f7ff examples/tyrannosaurus_rex_60cm/platform/design.json:18; tyrannosaurus_rex_intermediate.xml:4,266; environments/tyrannosaurus_rex/intermediate/env.py:43,201-206).
(2) IMU sampling, the trained gravity filter, foot-load sensing and the 1.40 Hz phase oscillator, i.e. every actor input (BEHAVIOR_SPEC.md:35; robot_walking.py:10-33; env.py:241).
(3) The locomotion policy itself. It is an 8.8k-parameter MLP, ~20-50 us per step on a 600 MHz M7. On the MCU, walking never depends on Linux scheduling, and a Linux crash still ends in the controlled stop-then-stand the spec requires (BEHAVIOR_SPEC.md:52). SIM_TO_REAL_PLAN.md:53 already notes a ~200k-parameter MLP at 100 Hz fits a microcontroller.
(4) The receiving bridge for velocity requests: <= 200 ms expiry, arbitration, clipping to the trained command range and rate limits (ROS2.md:29; env.py:125-129).
(5) The independent watchdog chain: Linux-alive heartbeat, MCU hardware watchdog, drive communication faults, bus voltage/current, ToF near-range and floor-drop stop, e-stop sense and traction-disconnect drive. PR #662 explicitly leaves these as commissioning tasks (ROS2.md:31; platform/engineering/README.md:85, 'Retain a hardware traction disconnect independent of Jetson').
None of this exists yet. The only watchdogs are inside the Linux Python process (controller.py:159-166; ros2_node.py:151-185), and a process crash publishes nothing (ros2_node.py:290-291 sends zero only on a clean shutdown).

CAN run on Linux (soft real time at 10-40 Hz; any miss ends in a stop):
- camera/depth ingestion and time alignment;
- person detection and re-ID (on the camera VPU or a host NPU/GPU, not the CPU);
- tracking, selection and ambiguity logic;
- the obstacle grid and local planner;
- TwistStamped generation (25 ms timer);
- the operator web server and streaming;
- logging, voice and mapping.
These already carry Linux-side deadlines: 60 ms skew, 200 ms request age, 0.35 s stale data, 0.80 s operator lease (ros2_node.py:20-22; controller.py:125,162-165).

Option: the policy may run on Linux only with PREEMPT_RT, a C++ ONNX Runtime loop on an isolated core, and the MCU enforcing a 2-period (40 ms) output timeout with a fallback stance controller.

The repository's earlier architectures already split the work this way (docs/hardware/HARDWARE_BOM.md:60-62,162-163: Pi 5 + ESP32 or Teensy 4.1 CAN master; docs/hardware/RAPTOR_SCALING_AND_ALTERNATIVES.md:271-273,491-492: deterministic Teensy motor/safety controller plus a separate Jetson-class computer).
